WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.382
A data race occurs when multiple threads access the same mutable object without proper synchronization and at least one access modifies it.

2
00:00:07.382 --> 00:00:10.402
In C++, that is more than a timing bug.

3
00:00:10.402 --> 00:00:16.107
It can create undefined behavior, which means the program is no longer operating under a reliable contract.

4
00:00:16.107 --> 00:00:20.134
The defensive response is not to add locks everywhere after the fact.

5
00:00:20.134 --> 00:00:22.147
Start by reducing shared mutable state.

6
00:00:22.147 --> 00:00:31.543
If data can be immutable, passed by value, owned by one thread, or moved through a queue, the design is often easier to reason about than a shared

7
00:00:31.543 --> 00:00:33.221
object protected by scattered synchronization.

8
00:00:33.221 --> 00:00:35.234
Thread confinement is a powerful simplifier.

9
00:00:35.234 --> 00:00:44.965
When a piece of state is only accessed by one worker, one event loop, or one owner at a time, review can focus on transfer points instead of every

10
00:00:44.965 --> 00:00:46.643
line that touches the object.

11
00:00:46.643 --> 00:00:49.663
When state must be shared, make the sharing visible.

12
00:00:49.663 --> 00:00:55.703
Avoid hidden global state, untracked singletons, caches with unclear locking policy, and references that quietly cross thread boundaries.

13
00:00:55.703 --> 00:01:01.744
A reviewer should not have to search the whole program to learn what else can mutate an object.

14
00:01:01.744 --> 00:01:04.428
Passing tests once does not prove concurrency safety.

15
00:01:04.428 --> 00:01:09.462
Scheduling changes with CPU count, compiler options, instrumentation, logging, timing, load, and operating-system behavior.

16
00:01:09.462 --> 00:01:14.495
A race that does not appear during a normal test run can still be present.

17
00:01:14.495 --> 00:01:17.515
Secure design should first ask whether sharing is necessary.

18
00:01:17.515 --> 00:01:23.891
If it is, define the owner, the protection mechanism, the allowed operations, and the lifetime of the shared object.

19
00:01:23.891 --> 00:01:27.918
That clarity turns concurrency review from guesswork into a concrete engineering check.

20
00:01:27.918 --> 00:01:31.609
The durable habit is to prefer simple ownership over clever coordination.

21
00:01:31.609 --> 00:01:38.320
Shared mutable state is sometimes necessary, but every shared object should earn its place and have a documented protection story.
